    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "points = [[0,0],[2,2],[3,10],[5,2],[7,0]]   ->  20\npoints = [[3,12],[-2,5],[-4,1]]             ->  18\npoints = [[0,0]]                            ->  0    (nothing to connect)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: minimum spanning tree"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Every pair of points can be joined, so this is a **complete** weighted graph. Connecting everything as cheaply as possible means choosing a **minimum spanning tree** (MST): a set of edges that reaches every vertex, has no cycle (a cycle always has one edge you could drop to save money), and costs the least. A tree on n vertices always has exactly **n - 1** edges."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Both classic algorithms are **greedy**, and both rest on one fact, the **cut property**: split the vertices into any two groups; the cheapest edge **crossing** between them is always safe to take. Taking it can never stop you from reaching the cheapest total."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Kruskal** — sort all edges from cheapest to most expensive and take each one that joins two different groups, using Union-Find (Pattern 7) to tell. An edge inside one group would close a cycle: skip it.",
        "**Prim** — grow one tree from any vertex. Each step, add the outside vertex that is **cheapest to attach**, and let it offer cheaper links to the vertices still outside."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Kruskal: cheapest edges first, with Union-Find"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "List all n(n - 1) / 2 pairs, sort them by cost, and add each edge whose ends are in different groups. Stop at n - 1 edges."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function minCostConnectPoints(points) {\n  const n = points.length;\n  const edges = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n      edges.push([Math.abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0]) + Math.abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1]), i, j]);\n    }\n  }\n  edges.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);          // cheapest edges first\n  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);\n  const find = (x) => {\n    while (parent[x] !== x) {\n      parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];\n      x = parent[x];\n    }\n    return x;\n  };\n  let total = 0, used = 0;\n  for (const [w, i, j] of edges) {\n    if (used === n - 1) break;                // a tree on n points has n - 1 edges\n    const a = find(i), b = find(j);\n    if (a === b) continue;                    // already connected: this edge would close a loop\n    parent[a] = b;\n    total += w;\n    used++;\n  }\n  return total;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def min_cost_connect_points(points):\n    n = len(points)\n    edges = sorted(                       # cheapest edges first\n        (abs(x1 - x2) + abs(y1 - y2), i, j)\n        for i, (x1, y1) in enumerate(points)\n        for j, (x2, y2) in enumerate(points)\n        if i < j\n    )\n    parent = list(range(n))\n\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n\n    total = used = 0\n    for w, i, j in edges:\n        if used == n - 1:\n            break                         # a tree on n points has n - 1 edges\n        a, b = find(i), find(j)\n        if a == b:\n            continue                      # already connected: this edge would close a loop\n        parent[a] = b\n        total += w\n        used += 1\n    return total"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] parent;\n\nint minCostConnectPoints(int[][] points) {\n    int n = points.length;\n    List<int[]> edges = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)\n            edges.add(new int[]{Math.abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0]) + Math.abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1]), i, j});\n    edges.sort((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));   // cheapest edges first\n    parent = new int[n];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;\n    int total = 0, used = 0;\n    for (int[] e : edges) {\n        if (used == n - 1) break;                // a tree on n points has n - 1 edges\n        int a = find(e[1]), b = find(e[2]);\n        if (a == b) continue;                    // already connected: this edge would close a loop\n        parent[a] = b;\n        total += e[0];\n        used++;\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n\nint find(int x) {\n    while (parent[x] != x) {\n        parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];\n        x = parent[x];\n    }\n    return x;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int minCostConnectPoints(vector<vector<int>>& points) {\n    int n = points.size();\n    vector<array<int, 3>> edges;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)\n            edges.push_back({abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0]) + abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1]), i, j});\n    sort(edges.begin(), edges.end());            // cheapest edges first\n    vector<int> parent(n);\n    iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);\n    auto find = [&](int x) {\n        while (parent[x] != x) {\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];\n            x = parent[x];\n        }\n        return x;\n    };\n    int total = 0, used = 0;\n    for (auto& [w, i, j] : edges) {\n        if (used == n - 1) break;                // a tree on n points has n - 1 edges\n        int a = find(i), b = find(j);\n        if (a == b) continue;                    // already connected: this edge would close a loop\n        parent[a] = b;\n        total += w;\n        used++;\n    }\n    return total;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n² log n), space O(n²).** There are about n² / 2 edges; sorting them dominates. Storing them all takes O(n²) memory."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Prim: grow one tree"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Keep `best[v]`, the cheapest edge from the tree to each outside point. Each round, pick the outside point with the smallest `best`, add it, and let it lower the `best` of the points still outside. In a complete graph a simple array scan beats a heap: no edge list, no sorting."
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "min-cost-connect-points",
      "caption": "The tree grows by its cheapest link each round; grey lines show each outside point's best link."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function minCostConnectPoints(points) {\n  const n = points.length;\n  const dist = (i, j) => Math.abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0]) + Math.abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1]);\n  const inTree = new Array(n).fill(false);\n  const best = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);   // cheapest edge from the tree to each point\n  best[0] = 0;\n  let total = 0;\n  for (let added = 0; added < n; added++) {\n    let u = -1;\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {             // the closest point not yet in the tree\n      if (!inTree[i] && (u === -1 || best[i] < best[u])) u = i;\n    }\n    inTree[u] = true;\n    total += best[u];                         // join it by its cheapest edge\n    for (let v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n      if (!inTree[v] && dist(u, v) < best[v]) best[v] = dist(u, v);   // u may offer a cheaper edge\n    }\n  }\n  return total;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def min_cost_connect_points(points):\n    n = len(points)\n\n    def dist(i, j):\n        return abs(points[i][0] - points[j][0]) + abs(points[i][1] - points[j][1])\n\n    in_tree = [False] * n\n    best = [float(\"inf\")] * n             # cheapest edge from the tree to each point\n    best[0] = 0\n    total = 0\n    for _ in range(n):\n        u = min((i for i in range(n) if not in_tree[i]), key=lambda i: best[i])   # the closest point outside\n        in_tree[u] = True\n        total += best[u]                  # join it by its cheapest edge\n        for v in range(n):\n            if not in_tree[v] and dist(u, v) < best[v]:\n                best[v] = dist(u, v)      # u may offer a cheaper edge\n    return total"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int minCostConnectPoints(int[][] points) {\n    int n = points.length;\n    boolean[] inTree = new boolean[n];\n    int[] best = new int[n];                     // cheapest edge from the tree to each point\n    Arrays.fill(best, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n    best[0] = 0;\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int added = 0; added < n; added++) {\n        int u = -1;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {            // the closest point not yet in the tree\n            if (!inTree[i] && (u == -1 || best[i] < best[u])) u = i;\n        }\n        inTree[u] = true;\n        total += best[u];                        // join it by its cheapest edge\n        for (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n            int d = Math.abs(points[u][0] - points[v][0]) + Math.abs(points[u][1] - points[v][1]);\n            if (!inTree[v] && d < best[v]) best[v] = d;   // u may offer a cheaper edge\n        }\n    }\n    return total;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int minCostConnectPoints(vector<vector<int>>& points) {\n    int n = points.size();\n    vector<bool> inTree(n, false);\n    vector<int> best(n, INT_MAX);                // cheapest edge from the tree to each point\n    best[0] = 0;\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int added = 0; added < n; added++) {\n        int u = -1;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {            // the closest point not yet in the tree\n            if (!inTree[i] && (u == -1 || best[i] < best[u])) u = i;\n        }\n        inTree[u] = true;\n        total += best[u];                        // join it by its cheapest edge\n        for (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n            int d = abs(points[u][0] - points[v][0]) + abs(points[u][1] - points[v][1]);\n            if (!inTree[v] && d < best[v]) best[v] = d;   // u may offer a cheaper edge\n        }\n    }\n    return total;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n²), space O(n).** n rounds, each scanning all n points twice. Distances are computed on the fly, so nothing but two arrays is stored."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Kruskal: cheapest edges first, with Union-Find",
          "time": "O(n² log n)",
          "timeWhy": "There are about **n²/2** edges, and **sorting** them dominates.",
          "space": "O(n²)",
          "spaceWhy": "**All the edges** are stored to be sorted."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Prim: grow one tree",
          "time": "O(n²)",
          "timeWhy": "**n** rounds, each scanning all **n** points.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "Distances are computed on the fly: only **two arrays** of n."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For a **complete** graph like this one, array-based Prim is the best fit. For a **sparse** graph given as an edge list, Kruskal (O(E log E)) or Prim with a heap (O(E log V)) is the usual choice."
    },
